% ============================================================================
% muscle-types — the three types of muscle tissue and the ONE distinctive
% characteristic the source assigns each one (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A rung a MEDICINE agent reasons UP from: below the four basic tissue types
% (see tissue-types.adj) the MUSCLE tissue splits into exactly THREE kinds —
% skeletal, smooth, and cardiac — and the source separates them by a single
% telling trait. "Skeletal muscle is under voluntary control; smooth muscle is
% involuntary; cardiac muscle is the one with intercalated disks." Before you
% can reason about a muscle biopsy, a heart, or a gut wall you must first know
% WHICH of the three muscle-tissue types you are looking at. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? muscle_trait(cardiac, $Trait)      % intercalated_disks
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `muscle_trait(muscle, trait)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the trait
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word
% that is not one of these three muscle-tissue types (it never invents a trait).
%
% Each of the three muscle-tissue types and the ONE distinctive characteristic
% the source states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     muscle type   distinctive characteristic   a beginner's cue
%     -----------   --------------------------   ------------------------------
%     skeletal      voluntary                    the muscle you move on purpose
%     smooth        involuntary                  gut & vessel walls, not willed
%     cardiac       intercalated_disks           the disks unique to heart muscle
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a trait and recall its muscle type:
%
%     ? muscle_trait($Muscle, involuntary)   % smooth
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three muscle-tissue types — `bone`, `tendon`,
% `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a muscle recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a trait. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the muscle→trait pairs
% it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `trait` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% distinctive characteristic the source EXPLICITLY states for that muscle type,
% and the three values are kept DISTINCT. The source calls both smooth and
% cardiac muscle involuntary, so `involuntary` could not tell those two apart;
% cardiac's row therefore uses `intercalated_disks`, the trait the source names
% as UNIQUE to cardiac muscle ("The presence of intercalated disks distinguishes
% cardiac muscle as unique among the three types" — the source's own summary),
% so every value stays literally checkable against its own quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every muscle→trait pair
% below was read out of a fetched NCI SEER Training Modules page, and any pair
% whose trait could not be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair,
% with the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states
% it:
%
%   skeletal → voluntary
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, multinucleated, striated, and
%      under voluntary control."
%
%   smooth → involuntary
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped, have a single, centrally located
%      nucleus, and lack striations. They are called involuntary muscles."
%
%   cardiac → intercalated_disks
%     https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html
%     (NCI SEER Training Modules — U.S. National Cancer Institute)
%     "Cardiac muscle has branching fibers, one nucleus per cell, striations,
%      and intercalated disks."
%
% (The three-type framing itself is stated on the same SEER page —
% "There are three types of muscle tissue: Skeletal muscle ... Smooth muscle ...
% Cardiac muscle ..." — another span of the same primary U.S. government
% teaching resource.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the SEER statement that fixes the
% first row (skeletal → voluntary). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and
% verbatim span is listed above, so each trait remains independently checkable.
% All sources are the NCI SEER Training Modules, a primary U.S. government
% (National Cancer Institute) teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table muscle_trait {
    columns muscle, trait

    row (skeletal, voluntary)
    row (smooth, involuntary)
    row (cardiac, intercalated_disks)

    source "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, multinucleated, striated, and under voluntary control."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
